ney": -1.2}, "rating_margin": -12.89, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.27, "baseline_margin": -8.53, "analyst_adjustment": 0.03, "key_factors": ["R+7 district with well-funded incumbent Pete Stauber running again", "Large fundraising gap: Stauber has raised $2.0M to Swanson's $317K and holds a $1.2M-to-$90K cash edge", "Dem-favorable national environment offsets part of the district's Republican lean", "No public general-election polling; raters split between Likely R and Solid/Safe R"], "rationale": "Stauber's incumbency, the district's seven-point Republican lean, and his roughly six-to-one fundraising advantage make him a clear favorite, with markets pricing Democratic chances near one in seven. A national environment favoring Democrats this cycle keeps the race from reaching Safe R territory.", "watch": "A decisively Democratic national wave, or late polling showing Swanson within single digits, could push this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pete Stauber (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Rep. Pete Stauber seeks another term in a northeastern Minnesota district that leans Republican by about seven points at the presidential level. He has out-raised Democratic nominee Trina Swanson roughly six to one and holds a large cash advantage, while outside groups have spent almost nothing in the race. Expert raters split between Likely R and Solid or Safe R, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-seven chance.\n\nThe main counterweight is a national environment that has favored Democrats this cycle, which keeps the race short of Safe R. No public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, expert ratings, and market pricing. Stauber remains a clear favorite, though the district's blue-collar Democratic history means a strong wave year could narrow the margin more than current ratings suggest.", "